{
 "description": "CRAM Sudoku-Extreme solve rate against a per-puzzle flop cap, from the saved test records (no reruns).\n\n  .venv/bin/python experiments/compute_curve_export.py      (on a CPU allocation; reads about 60 MB of records)\n\nEach test record experiments/runs/CRAM-S/full/test/cram0-hb-fit-s{0,1,2}.json (the model CRAM0 with the stopping threshold fitted\nfrom the training solutions, E <= tau + 1e-3) holds, for each of the 412,786 unseen puzzles, the flop at which its search\nstopped and whether the answer is correct (cap 4e11). A puzzle's search follows a fixed random sequence, so with a lower\ncap b it stops at the same point if it used at most b flop; otherwise it would hit the cap and return its lowest-energy\nboard, which this export counts as unsolved below the records' cap (a slight underestimate). At the records' cap the\nreturned board is the record's, so a run that hit the cap with the right board counts as solved there, as in the table\nrecord.\nThe mean flop at cap b is mean(min(flop, b)); p99 is the 99th-percentile per-puzzle flop at cap b. Two puzzle sets: all 412,786 unseen puzzles,\nand full-all rows 10,000-19,999 (EqR's first10k), the subset of the baseline compute sweeps (results/compute-sweep/).\nOperation counts: each puzzle's count is rebuilt from its record under the arithmetic-only\nconvention (cram/opcount.cram0_sudoku: steps x C x counted move + start + checks); the records' cap of 4e11 old\nflop becomes, per seed, the count of its move cap T_cap.\nDimensions: p = puzzles, s = seeds.\nWrites results/cram-anytime/sudoku-extreme.json.",
